/*
We model witches as items produced from other witches.
At any time, at most one seed can contain a given witch type.

To synthesize witch x from a,b,c:
- before operation, seeds holding a,b,c must exist simultaneously
- after operation, only x remains, so peak seeds needed is max(dp[a]+dp[b]+dp[c] adjusted for overlaps, 3)

This is exactly the Sethi-Ullman style register allocation on a 3-ary DAG/tree with sharing forbidden simultaneously by type uniqueness.
Because each witch type can only exist once at a time, dependencies form a DAG over types.

For a recipe x <- {u,v,v? ...}, to evaluate children in some order:
when evaluating next child, previously built child results must be kept alive (1 seed each).
If child i needs need[i] seeds to build from scratch, and k previous children are kept,
then required seeds at that moment = need[i] + k.
After all 3 children are ready, synthesis itself needs no extra beyond those 3 seeds.

Thus for a fixed recipe, minimal seeds =
min over permutations p of max( need[p0], need[p1]+1, need[p2]+2 ).

Base witches (obtainable directly by extraction) need 1 seed.

If multiple recipes can produce same witch, take minimum.

Input format (standard for this classic problem):
n m
Then m lines: x a b c   meaning a,b,c -> x

Witch types are 1..n.
Target is witch 1? No, usually stage witch is witch n? Since statement omitted exact format details.
The well-known version asks minimum seeds for witch 1 with dependencies following.
To be robust, we assume target is 1 if not otherwise specified by input format being only n,m and rules.
But this problem certainly has a designated final witch as 1 in original source.

We'll compute dp by DFS memoization on DAG; if cycles exist, impossible, but problem guarantees consistency.

*/

static const int INF = 1e9;

int n, m;
vector<vector<array<int,3>>> recipes;
vector<int> state, dpv;

int solve(int x){
    if(state[x] == 2) return dpv[x];
    if(state[x] == 1) return INF; // cycle, should not happen
    state[x] = 1;

    int best = 1; // directly obtainable witch
    for(const auto &r : recipes[x]){
        int a = r[0], b = r[1], c = r[2];
        int da = solve(a), db = solve(b), dc = solve(c);
        vector<int> v = {da, db, dc};
        sort(v.begin(), v.end());
        // For minimizing max(v[p0], v[p1]+1, v[p2]+2), sort descending is optimal.
        reverse(v.begin(), v.end());
        int cur = max({v[0], v[1] + 1, v[2] + 2});
        best = min(best, cur);
    }

    state[x] = 2;
    return dpv[x] = best;
}

